## Kepano knowledge rules

Universal note-keeping rules. If you keep one canonical copy of these rules elsewhere and sync it into each vault, the sentinel comments mark the managed region and edits inside the pair are overwritten on the next sync. Otherwise treat this block as yours to edit.

### Categories vs folders

- Categories drive filtering via Bases; folders carry only the coarse split between own writing, content by others, and external references.
- `[[Clippings]]` category ↔ `Clippings/` folder (loose coupling — categories sort, folders contain).
- `[[Inbox]]` category = "needs processing". File stays where it is until processed.
- Personal-writing categories (`[[Essays]]`, `[[Thoughts]]`, `[[Journals]]`) live at vault root.

### Wikilink conventions

When creating or editing notes, add wikilinks following these rules:

1. **First occurrence only** — link the first mention of a term in the body text, not every instance.
2. **Skip zones** — never add wikilinks inside:
   - YAML frontmatter (between `---` markers)
   - Code blocks (between `` ``` `` markers)
   - Headings (lines starting with `#`)
   - URLs
   - Existing wikilinks or markdown links
3. **Whole word matching** — don't link substrings (e.g., don't link "Integration" inside "Disintegration").
4. **People** — use display name format: `[[Full Name|First Name]]`.
5. **Don't self-link** — a note should not wikilink to itself.
6. **Longest match first** — when two targets overlap (e.g., "Total Cost of Ownership" vs "Cost"), prefer the longer match.
7. **What earns a link** — a passing mention qualifies: parenthetical lists, quote attributions and yardstick uses ("the next Amazon") all link on first mention. Three disqualifiers only: **sense mismatch** (the surface must carry the sense the target note carries — common-noun titles link the concept under discussion, while instances of it stay bare); **fragment of a longer term** (a phrase naming a different entity keeps its parts bare: "leveraged finance", "Carolingian Renaissance"; a compound whose head concept IS the target links: "the finance industry", "AI datacenters"); **rhetorical stand-in** (aphorism, personification, idiom — "History does not punish optimism" stays bare). Quotation marks alone disqualify nothing.

### Property standards

#### Naming rules

- **Lowercase property keys are mandatory**: `source:` not `Source:`, `created:` not `CREATED:`.
- **Never uppercase YAML property keys**: no `TITLE:`, `SOURCE:`, `Created:`, or `kindle-bookId:` unless preserving an external import field intentionally.
- **Lowercase snake_case for multi-word keys**: `prep_time`, `cost_tier`, `weight_g`, not `prepTime`, `Prep Time`, `prep-time`, or `PREP_TIME`.
- **Only property keys are constrained**: values may use normal casing (e.g. `status: "In Progress"`, `region: "North America"`).
- **Plural for lists**: `categories:`, `topics:`, `tags:`.
- **Singular for single values**: `author:`, `rating:`.
- **Before saving frontmatter**: scan the keys between `---` fences and convert accidental uppercase or camelCase keys to lowercase snake_case.

#### Standard properties

| Property | Type | Usage |
|----------|------|-------|
| `categories` | list | Top-level organisation (wikilinks) |
| `topics` | list | Thematic tags (wikilinks) |
| `author` | single or list | Creator (wikilink) |
| `people` | list | Related people (wikilinks) |
| `source` | single | Origin reference (wikilink) |
| `type` | single | Content type (wikilink, plural: `[[Books]]`) |
| `genre` | single or list | Media genre (wikilink) |
| `description` | text | One-sentence summary of the note |
| `notes` | text | Additional commentary or observations |
| `rating` | number | 1-7 scale |
| `created` | date | YYYY-MM-DD |
| `published` | date | YYYY-MM-DD |
| `date` | date | Event or occurrence date |
| `last_reviewed` | datetime | Last reviewed or updated date |
| `modified` | date | Last modified date |
| `status` | text | e.g., `published`, `draft` |
| `project` | text | Project name |

#### Cross-category reusability

- `genre` shared across Books, Movies, Shows, Podcasts.
- `author` shared across Books, Articles, Quotes.
- `type` uses wikilinks: `type: "[[Books]]"`.

